export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>User interfaces are a unique feature in video games, because when you think about it, no other art forms have such things. Since video games are an interactive medium, it is necessary to have these communicative devices to inform players of executable functions. But there is an inherent side effect: interfaces remind people that they are playing a game. While that isn't at all a bad thing, some games want to go above and beyond with their quality of immersion. So, they opt for diegetic UI.</p>\n<p>Simply put, diegetic UI is the type of interfaces that exists within the game world. For example, when you open a map, and your character take out a physical map instead of fading to a menu, that's diegetic. But it's important to note that diegetic UI doesn't always have to be physical. The key distinction is context: so long as the UI elements are contextualized within the game world, it can be considered diegetic. For example, the holographic map in <em>Star Wars Jedi: Fallen Order</em> is still a diegetic element.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/0f09b24b5e3b6b3bd4bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/0f09b24b5e3b6b3bd4bf.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/70f0c83120499ef07c97.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/70f0c83120499ef07c97.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/48f2d427883bf34ac74e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/48f2d427883bf34ac74e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:293px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Oftentimes, games benefit from diegetic UI when they strive for a grounded, realistic experience. The \"inspect\" feature in <em>Red Dead Redemption</em><em> 2</em> allows players to closely examine pickable props, making the world feel more intimate.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Some games will use a combination of regular UI and diegetic elements to convey information more efficiently to players. For example, unlocking a safe in <em>The Last of Us: Part II</em> will prompt the player character to manually turn the knob according to the player's inputs.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b35412debe6098649921.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b35412debe6098649921.webp\" width=\"734\" height=\"407\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/38477132ff43498430dc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/38477132ff43498430dc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:293px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/7d1398ac74604bce3bc7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/7d1398ac74604bce3bc7.webp\" width=\"850\" height=\"473\" style=\"--source-width:293px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Perhaps it is impossible to discuss diegetic UI without bringing up the name <em>Dead Space</em>. This game is still one of, if not, the best exhibitions of the feature. Some even argue it <em>is</em> still the best one to date because every interaction in this game is contextualized within its universe. The game even went as far as abandoning traditional heads-up display, instead opting to show health, ammo count, and energy gauge on the character's model. And as you can see in the GIF, the inventory menu is a holographic projection used by the player character himself. You see the world exactly through his eyes.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Despite the apparent increase in immersion, this feature is not an easy implementation, for it requires extra work from various departments. On the surface level, the prop artists have to create high-fidelity models for interactable objects; then the animators have to author detailed animations for these interactions. In short, it requires multiple disciplines to collaborate closely.</p>\n<p>Although game development is always a collaborative endeavor, not every game has to go this extent to immerse players, which is why diegetic UI is not the go-to option in the industry. But it's hard to deny how cool this type of UI looks when we see it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/67ab6868e4d0a3a90525.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/67ab6868e4d0a3a90525.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The mission list in <em>Metro 2033</em>.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
